<.shell title="Open in maps" current={:open_in_maps}>
  <h1>Open in maps</h1>
  <p class="lead">A place in the account's maps app.</p>

  <div class="demo max-w-sm">
    <dl>
      <dt>Address</dt>
      <dd>
        Murrin Provincial Park, Squamish<.copy_button
          id="open-in-maps-demo-copy-address"
          value="Murrin Provincial Park, Squamish"
          label="Copy address"
        />
      </dd>
      <dt>Latitude and longitude</dt>
      <dd>
        49.64310,-123.20890<.copy_button
          id="open-in-maps-demo-copy-coordinate"
          value="49.64310,-123.20890"
          label="Copy latitude and longitude"
        />
        <.open_in_maps
          id="open-in-maps-demo"
          coordinate="49.64310,-123.20890"
          label="Rope rescue practice"
        />
      </dd>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use</h2>
  <p>
    Under an activity's latitude and longitude, or its address when it has none, and under a member's address.
  </p>

  <h2>When not to use</h2>
  <p>
    In a list row that is already a link, such as a <a href={page_path(:row_link)}>row link</a>: a link cannot hold another.
  </p>

  <h2>In the app</h2>
  <p>
    <code>&lt;.open_in_maps id="…" app=&#123;@current_user.maps_app&#125; coordinate=&#123;…&#125; address=&#123;…&#125; label=&#123;…&#125; /&gt;</code>. It opens Google Maps, or Apple Maps when the person chose it on the Account page.
  </p>
  <p>
    With a latitude and longitude it pins that spot, and Apple Maps labels the pin with <code>label</code>. Google Maps cannot. Without one it searches for the address. Never label a member's address with their name.
  </p>
  <p>
    Put a <a href={page_path(:copy_button)}>copy button</a> after the value, for a GPS app.
  </p>
</.shell>
